      "lineNbr": 269,
      "author": {
        "id": 5046
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-01-31T21:42:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e OK . Thanks lance. As in the above paragraph it is said that the\n \u003e service will attach both the tokens, the user\u0027s token and its own.\n \u003e So does that conclude that each service is having an separate token\n \u003e ?\n\nKind of, yes. It\u0027s not that each service has their own token, but credentials they use to authenticate for tokens they can use to interact with other services in the deployment.\n\nFor example, if nova needs something in glance or cinder, nova can generate a token (e.g., a service token) to call the appropriate glance or cinder API for that information. In that example, nova would be using a service token to make a request to another service. Typically, it\u0027s common to see service users created when installing a new service (this is the service user) [0].\n\nService credentials are set in each service\u0027s configuration file so they can programmatically grab tokens when they need to and use various openstack clients.\n\n[0] https://docs.openstack.org/nova/latest/install/controller-install-ubuntu.html (step 3)",
      "parentUuid": "9fdfeff1_74e339da",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 269,
